# PART 5 — GLOBAL SECURITY GOVERNANCE MASTER ENABLE/DISABLE

## 6. Mandatory global master switch

Implement one authoritative Global Security Governance Enable/Disable control.

### Location

Governance & System Settings → Security Governance → Security Dashboard / Global Baseline Policy.

Display:

- ZERO-TRUST SECURITY GOVERNANCE.
- Global Status: ENABLED / DISABLED.
- Master Enable/Disable control.
- Current policy version.
- Last modified by.
- Last modified date and time.
- Approval status.
- Effective company and branch scope.
- Policy propagation status.

This control must govern the enforcement of all policies under the Zero-Trust Security Governance hierarchy.

## 7. Global ENABLED behaviour

When enabled:

- Activate approved Global Baseline Security Policy.
- Enforce applicable Company policies.
- Enforce applicable Branch policies.
- Enforce applicable Module policies.
- Enforce configured Page/Screen policies.
- Enforce authorized User-specific policies.
- Activate configured browser governance.
- Activate configured visual and data protection controls.
- Activate configured clipboard and export controls.
- Activate configured session and blackout controls.
- Activate security event monitoring.
- Activate incident detection.
- Activate configured compliance notification workflows.

Only approved, published and effective policies should be enforced.

## 8. Global DISABLED behaviour

When disabled:

- Stop enforcement of policies governed by this master switch.
- Stop new policy-triggered blackouts caused solely by disabled policies.
- Stop generating new violations solely because disabled policy conditions were encountered.
- Preserve existing security incidents.
- Preserve existing evidence.
- Preserve all audit history.
- Do not automatically release existing security locks.
- Do not remove existing approvals or restrictions.
- Do not disable mandatory platform security safeguards.

The following must remain active even when Global Governance is disabled:

- Authentication.
- Authorization.
- RBAC.
- User identity verification.
- Company isolation.
- Branch isolation.
- API authorization.
- Audit logging.
- Mandatory platform security controls.
- Existing incident and evidence records.

Display this warning:

"Global Security Governance is disabled. Configured visual and data protection enforcement policies are currently inactive. Authentication, authorization, RBAC, audit logging and mandatory platform security controls remain active."

## 9. Policy inheritance rules

1. Global Disabled disables descendant governance enforcement, except mandatory platform safeguards.
2. Company Disabled disables applicable enforcement within that company.
3. Branch Disabled disables applicable enforcement within that branch.
4. Module Disabled disables applicable enforcement within that module.
5. Page/Screen and User overrides require authorization from the parent policy.
6. A child policy cannot activate enforcement when its parent is disabled.
7. A child policy cannot bypass mandatory parent restrictions.
8. Effective policy state must be calculated server-side.
9. Frontend state must reflect verified backend state.

## 10. Maker-Checker approval

Global Enable/Disable is a critical administrative action.

Require:

- Authorized administrator to initiate.
- Independent authorized Checker to approve.
- Mandatory reason.
- Change reference.
- Effective date/time.
- Optional expiry date/time for temporary disablement.
- Confirmation before execution.
- Immutable audit record.

The initiator cannot approve their own request.

Reuse the existing KOSH Maker-Checker and RBAC infrastructure.

## 11. Emergency disable

Provide a separate, permission-controlled Emergency Disable mechanism.

Require:

- Emergency privilege.
- Incident or change reference.
- Mandatory business justification.
- Affected scope.
- Initiator identity.
- Start time.
- Expiry time.
- Administrator notification.
- Independent post-action review.

Emergency disable must not bypass authentication, authorization, RBAC, tenant isolation or audit logging.

When the expiry time is reached, restore the previously approved policy state only if safe and authorized.

If the prior policy configuration is unsafe, retain a controlled state and escalate to the security administrator.

## 12. Policy propagation

After an approved change:

1. Validate authorization.
2. Persist the new policy version.
3. Invalidate relevant policy caches.
4. Propagate the new policy to applicable backend services.
5. Refresh active sessions according to policy.
6. Verify the effective state.
7. Record propagation results.
8. Notify configured administrators.

Do not display SUCCESS until the backend confirms the effective state.

If propagation fails, display PARTIALLY_APPLIED or FAILED, identify affected services and preserve the last known safe state.

## 13. Global dashboard

Display actual database/runtime values for:

- Global Governance Status.
- Active Company Policies.
